var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"api/metrics/#Metrics-from-ResilienceCore","page":"Metrics from ResilienceCore","text":"The resilience and stability metrics are defined in ResilienceCore.jl, on operators whose type says what they are, and re-exported here: resilience, isstable, reactivity, amplification_envelope, propagator, max_amplification, pulse_covariance, median_reactivity, median_displacement, median_max_amplification, median_resilience, sensitivity_matrix, press_sensitivity, total_sensitivity, stochastic_invariability, applicable_metrics and jacobian_profile.\n\nEach accepts a Jacobian directly, by way of its ContinuousJacobian(j); the median metrics then use pulses proportional to the Jacobian's equilibrium. A plain matrix J is analysed as ContinuousJacobian(J), or ContinuousJacobian(J, equilibrium) for the median metrics. jacobian_profile collects the eleven Jacobian-based metrics of Domínguez-García et al. (2019) in one record; ResilienceCore's metric catalogue lists them all.","title":"Metrics from ResilienceCore"},{"category":"section","location":"api/structural/#Structural-stability-and-sign-patterns","page":"Structural stability and sign patterns","text":"These analyses need only a matrix, so they apply to empirical coefficient matrices and sign patterns as well as to fitted models. The qualitative analyses are ResilienceCore's, applied to SignPattern(s).","title":"Structural stability and sign patterns"},{"category":"function","location":"api/structural/#CommunityMatrices.feasibility_domain","page":"Structural stability and sign patterns","text":"feasibility_domain([rng], a::AlphaMatrix; samples = 100_000)\n\nThe size of the feasibility domain of a (Saavedra et al. 2017): the fraction Ω of directions of the growth-rate vector r (uniform on the unit sphere) for which the interior equilibrium −A⁻¹ r is positive, estimated from samples random directions. Returns (Ω, Ω^(1/S), se), with se the Monte Carlo standard error of Ω. Ω^(1/S) is comparable across community sizes; for A = −I it is 1/2.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityMatrices.feasibility_domain"},{"category":"function","location":"api/structural/#CommunityMatrices.structural_vector","page":"Structural stability and sign patterns","text":"structural_vector(a::AlphaMatrix)\n\nThe centroid of the feasibility cone of a: the normalised sum of the unit columns of −A (Saavedra et al. 2017). Growth-rate vectors along it are the most robustly feasible.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityMatrices.structural_vector"},{"category":"function","location":"api/structural/#CommunityMatrices.feasibility_angle","page":"Structural stability and sign patterns","text":"feasibility_angle(a::AlphaMatrix)\n\nThe angle in degrees between the growth-rate vector a.r and the structural_vector of a: a measure of how far the community is from the centre of its feasibility domain (larger means closer to losing feasibility).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityMatrices.feasibility_angle"},{"category":"method","location":"api/structural/#ResilienceCore.SignPattern-Tuple{SignedDigraph}","page":"Structural stability and sign patterns","text":"SignPattern(s::SignedDigraph)\n\nThe sign pattern of s as a ResilienceCore.SignPattern, labelled with its taxa when they are recorded. The qualitative analyses, quasi_sign_stability and press_sign_determinacy, are ResilienceCore's, applied to it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ResilienceCore.SignPattern"},{"category":"function","location":"api/structural/#ResilienceCore.quasi_sign_stability","page":"Structural stability and sign patterns","text":"quasi_sign_stability([rng], s::SignedDigraph; samples = 10_000)\n\nThe fraction of matrices with sign pattern s and magnitudes uniform on (0, 1) that are stable (every eigenvalue with negative real part): quasi-sign-stability (Allesina & Pascual 2008). A diagonal of zeros means no self-regulation. Returns (fraction, se). ResilienceCore's method on SignPattern(s).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ResilienceCore.quasi_sign_stability"},{"category":"function","location":"api/structural/#ResilienceCore.press_sign_determinacy","page":"Structural stability and sign patterns","text":"press_sign_determinacy([rng], s::SignedDigraph; samples = 10_000)\n\nQualitative press-perturbation analysis (Dambacher et al. 2003; the simulation approach of QPress): draw matrices with sign pattern s and magnitudes uniform on (0, 1), keep the stable ones, and for each compute the equilibrium response to a sustained increase in each species' growth, −J⁻¹. Returns (sign, determinacy, stable, labels): the most common sign of each response (an n×n matrix; row: responding species, column: pressed species; 0 when the response is structurally zero, below 1e-10 of the largest), the fraction of stable draws with that outcome, the fraction of draws that were stable, and the taxa (or nothing). ResilienceCore's method on SignPattern(s).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ResilienceCore.press_sign_determinacy"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/01_plant_communities/#Plant-communities:-structural-stability-and-sign-patterns-of-empirical-interaction-matrices","page":"Plant communities: structural stability and sign patterns","text":"Simon Frost\n\nOverview\nFrom published numbers to typed matrices\nStructural stability\nAgainst a null model\nSign patterns\nWhat growth rates would add\nCaveats\nReferences","title":"Plant communities: structural stability and sign patterns of empirical interaction matrices"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/01_plant_communities/#Overview","page":"Plant communities: structural stability and sign patterns","text":"Most resilience metrics need a model at an equilibrium: a Jacobian, which needs both the interaction coefficients and the growth rates. Empirical plant studies usually give only the first. This vignette asks what can still be said about stability from interaction matrices alone, using two published compilations:\n\nKinlock (2019): 25 complete plant interaction networks (3–8 species), measured as relative interaction indices (RII) against monoculture controls;\nAdler et al. (2018): 39 complete competition-coefficient matrices (2–6 species) from 19 field and greenhouse studies.\n\nBoth datasets are CC0 and ship with the package (data/kinlock2019, data/adler2018).\n\nTwo kinds of analysis need no growth rates:\n\nstructural stability: the share of growth-rate vectors for which all species can coexist (the feasibility domain; Saavedra et al. 2017);\nsign patterns: what the signs of the interactions alone imply for stability and for the response to a sustained press (Allesina & Pascual 2008; Dambacher et al. 2003).\n\nusing CommunityMatrices\nusing Random, Statistics, LinearAlgebra, Printf\nusing DataFrames\nusing Plots\ndata = joinpath(pkgdir(CommunityMatrices), \"data\")\nkinlock = read_kinlock(joinpath(data, \"kinlock2019\", \"Data_PlantInteractionNetworks.csv\"))\nadler = read_adler(joinpath(data, \"adler2018\", \"CompetitionRegressionData071517.csv\"))\n(kinlock = length(kinlock), adler = length(adler), adler_studies = length(unique(x.study for x in adler)))\n\n(kinlock = 25, adler = 39, adler_studies = 19)","title":"Overview"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/01_plant_communities/#From-published-numbers-to-typed-matrices","page":"Plant communities: structural stability and sign patterns","text":"The two datasets record different quantities, and neither is a Lotka–Volterra coefficient matrix as published.\n\nKinlock gives RII = (Pwith − Pwithout)/(Pwith + Pwithout) for each pair: a relative change in performance, bounded in [−1, 1], with a diagonal of 0 by construction. alpha_from_rii reads the RIIs as per-capita effects relative to a self-regulation of −1. This is an assumption, and the matrix records it.\nAdler gives competition coefficients whose sign convention varies by study. read_adler puts every study in the Lotka–Volterra convention (negative = competition), divides each row by its intraspecific coefficient so the diagonal is −1, and skips groups whose convention is free text or whose intraspecific coefficient is zero.\n\nNeither dataset has growth rates, so both readers set r = 1 and say so in the metadata.\n\nK = [alpha_from_rii(n) for n in kinlock]\nA = [x.alpha for x in adler]\na = K[16]                     # Pfeifer-Meister et al. 2008, four grassland species\nprintln(kinlock[16].study, \": \", join(taxa(a), \", \"))\ndisplay(round.(matrix(a); digits = 2))\nprintln(matrix_metadata(a).derivation)\n\nPfeifer-Meister et al. 2008: Danthonia californica, Deschampsia cespitosa, Festuca arundinacea, Lolium multiflorum\nKinlock 2019 RII as per-capita effects × 1.0, diagonal −1; r unknown, set to 1\n\n4×4 Matrix{Float64}:\n -1.0   -0.24   0.02  -0.2\n -0.01  -1.0   -0.08  -0.43\n  0.13   0.07  -1.0   -0.43\n  0.15   0.18   0.31  -1.0\n\nThe typed matrix keeps what kind of object it is. An AlphaMatrix is not a Jacobian: turning it into one (jacobian(a)) needs the abundances at equilibrium, and they need r.\n\nQuantity Needs Available here?\nfeasibility domain Ω, structural vector A yes\nquasi-sign-stability, press-response signs signs of A yes\nequilibrium N* = −A⁻¹r, isfeasible A, r no: depends on the placeholder r\nfeasibility_angle A, r no\nJacobian diag(N*) A and every metric on it (resilience, reactivity, amplification) A, r no","title":"From published numbers to typed matrices"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/01_plant_communities/#Structural-stability","page":"Plant communities: structural stability and sign patterns","text":"For a Lotka–Volterra community dNᵢ/dt = Nᵢ(rᵢ + Σⱼ Aᵢⱼ Nⱼ), all species coexist at an equilibrium when N* = −A⁻¹r is positive. The set of r for which this holds is a cone, the feasibility domain. Its size Ω is the fraction of random directions of r that fall inside it. feasibility_domain estimates Ω by Monte Carlo and also returns Ω^(1/S), which is comparable across community sizes: for A = −I, Ω^(1/S) = 1/2 at any S.\n\noffdiag(M) = [M[i, j] for i in axes(M, 1), j in axes(M, 2) if i != j]\nfunction summary_row(name, a)\n    o = offdiag(matrix(a))\n    fd = feasibility_domain(Xoshiro(2024), a; samples = 200_000)\n    return (dataset = name, S = nspecies(a), Ω = fd.Ω, Ωs = fd.Ω_scaled, mean_effect = mean(o),\n            competitive = mean(o .< 0))\nend\ntab = DataFrame(vcat([summary_row(\"Kinlock\", a) for a in K], [summary_row(\"Adler\", a) for a in A]))\ncombine(groupby(tab, :dataset), nrow => :n, :Ωs => median => :Ωs_median, :Ωs => minimum => :Ωs_min,\n        :Ωs => maximum => :Ωs_max, :competitive => median => :competitive_share,\n        [:mean_effect, :Ωs] => ((x, y) -> cor(x, y)) => :cor_effect_Ωs,\n        [:S, :Ωs] => ((x, y) -> cor(x, y)) => :cor_S_Ωs)\n\n<div><div style = \"float: left;\">2×8 DataFrame</div><div style = \"clear: both;\"></div></div><div class = \"data-frame\" style = \"overflow-x: scroll;\">\n\nRow dataset n Ωs_median Ωs_min Ωs_max competitive_share coreffectΩs corSΩs\n String Int64 Float64 Float64 Float64 Float64 Float64 Float64\n1 Kinlock 25 0.467477 0.0819036 0.699219 0.5 0.910411 -0.0301104\n2 Adler 39 0.265857 0.124826 0.629885 1.0 0.540815 -0.208641\n\n</div>\n\nKinlock communities have larger feasibility domains than Adler’s (median Ω^(1/S) 0.47 against 0.27).\nThe mean interaction explains most of the difference. Half of the Kinlock effects are facilitative, while Adler’s are, by selection, almost all competitive. Facilitation widens the feasibility cone and competition narrows it.\nRichness barely matters once Ω is scaled.\n\np = plot(xlabel = \"mean off-diagonal effect\", ylabel = \"Ω^(1/S)\", legend = :topleft, size = (650, 420))\nfor (nm, m) in ((\"Kinlock (RII)\", :circle), (\"Adler (normalised α)\", :diamond))\n    d = tab[startswith.(nm, tab.dataset), :]\n    scatter!(p, d.mean_effect, d.Ωs; label = nm, marker = m, markersize = 2 .+ d.S, alpha = 0.7)\nend\nhline!(p, [0.5]; ls = :dash, color = :gray, label = \"\")\np\n\n<div id=\"fig-effect\">\n\n(Image: )\n\nFigure 1: Scaled feasibility domain against the mean off-diagonal effect. The dashed line is Ω^(1/S) = 1/2, the value for non-interacting species.\n\n</div>","title":"Structural stability"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/01_plant_communities/#Against-a-null-model","page":"Plant communities: structural stability and sign patterns","text":"Is an observed community more structurally stable than other arrangements of the same interactions? The null keeps the diagonal and shuffles the observed off-diagonal effects among the off-diagonal positions. Each Ω is estimated from the same set of random directions (Xoshiro(2024)), so that observed and shuffled matrices are compared on common random numbers.\n\nOne case needs care. For two species the shuffle can only transpose the off-diagonal pair, and transposing leaves Ω unchanged (it mirrors the feasibility cone through the diagonal). The null is then uninformative, and any percentile is Monte Carlo noise around 1/2. This affects 25 of the 39 Adler matrices, so the comparison below uses communities of three or more species. For the first two-species matrix, the two estimates agree within their standard errors:\n\nfunction shuffled(rng, a)\n    M = copy(matrix(a))\n    idx = [CartesianIndex(i, j) for i in axes(M, 1), j in axes(M, 2) if i != j]\n    M[idx] = M[shuffle(rng, idx)]\n    return AlphaMatrix(M, a.r)\nend\npair = A[findfirst(a -> nspecies(a) == 2, A)]\nΩbig(a) = feasibility_domain(Xoshiro(7), a; samples = 2_000_000)\n(observed = Ωbig(pair).Ω, transposed = Ωbig(AlphaMatrix(permutedims(matrix(pair)), pair.r)).Ω, se = Ωbig(pair).se)\n\n(observed = 0.016342, transposed = 0.016525, se = 8.965193538345952e-5)\n\nΩcrn(a) = feasibility_domain(Xoshiro(2024), a; samples = 50_000).Ω_scaled\nfunction percentile(rng, a; shuffles = 200)\n    o = Ωcrn(a)\n    null = [Ωcrn(shuffled(rng, a)) for _ in 1:shuffles]\n    return mean(null .< o) + mean(null .== o) / 2\nend\nrng = Xoshiro(1)\ntab.percentile = [percentile(rng, a) for a in vcat(K, A)]\nbig = tab[tab.S .>= 3, :]\ncombine(groupby(big, :dataset), nrow => :n, :percentile => median => :median_percentile,\n        :percentile => (x -> count(>(0.5), x)) => :above_half)\n\n<div><div style = \"float: left;\">2×4 DataFrame</div><div style = \"clear: both;\"></div></div><div class = \"data-frame\" style = \"overflow-x: scroll;\">\n\nRow dataset n median_percentile above_half\n String Int64 Float64 Int64\n1 Kinlock 25 0.4825 12\n2 Adler 14 0.4175 5\n\n</div>\n\nq = plot(xlabel = \"community (sorted)\", ylabel = \"percentile among shuffles\", ylim = (0, 1),\n         legend = :topleft, size = (650, 380))\nfor (nm, m) in ((\"Kinlock\", :circle), (\"Adler\", :diamond))\n    d = sort(big[big.dataset .== nm, :], :percentile)\n    scatter!(q, range(0, 1; length = nrow(d)), d.percentile; label = nm, marker = m)\nend\nhline!(q, [0.5]; ls = :dash, color = :gray, label = \"\")\nq\n\n<div id=\"fig-null\">\n\n(Image: )\n\nFigure 2: Percentile of each community’s Ω^(1/S) among 200 shuffles of its own off-diagonal effects (communities of three or more species). Above 0.5: more structurally stable than the shuffles.\n\n</div>\n\nObserved plant communities are not more structurally stable than random arrangements of their own interactions. The median percentile is 0.48 for Kinlock and 0.42 for Adler, and the communities are spread across almost the whole range.\nWhat is arranged matters less than what there is. The mean effect, which the shuffle keeps, sets Ω; the positions of the effects, which the shuffle changes, do not move it systematically.","title":"Against a null model"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/01_plant_communities/#Sign-patterns","page":"Plant communities: structural stability and sign patterns","text":"The signs of a matrix alone constrain stability. SignedDigraph keeps only the signs, and the qualitative analyses draw random magnitudes uniform on (0, 1) for every non-zero entry, the diagonal included:\n\nquasi_sign_stability: the fraction of draws that are stable;\npress_sign_determinacy: among stable draws, the most common sign of each species’ response to a sustained increase in each species’ growth, and how often it occurs.\n\nqrng = Xoshiro(3)\ntab.qss = [quasi_sign_stability(qrng, SignedDigraph(a)).fraction for a in vcat(K, A)]\nsort!(unstack(combine(groupby(tab, [:dataset, :S]), :qss => (x -> round(median(x); digits = 2)) => :qss_median),\n              :S, :dataset, :qss_median), :S)\n\n<div><div style = \"float: left;\">7×3 DataFrame</div><div style = \"clear: both;\"></div></div><div class = \"data-frame\" style = \"overflow-x: scroll;\">\n\nRow S Kinlock Adler\n Int64 Float64? Float64?\n1 2 missing 0.5\n2 3 0.5 0.24\n3 4 0.45 0.15\n4 5 0.09 0.05\n5 6 0.03 0.01\n6 7 0.09 missing\n7 8 0.16 missing\n\n</div>\n\nA competing pair is stable in exactly half of the draws. With self-regulation dᵢ and competition cᵢⱼ all uniform on (0, 1), the pair is stable when d₁d₂ \\> c₁₂c₂₁, which by symmetry has probability 1/2. The 25 two-species Adler matrices sit there.\nLarger communities are less often stable. The median is 1/2 for Adler’s pairs and Kinlock’s three-species networks, and below 0.2 for five or more species in both datasets: each extra species adds feedback loops that a random draw can make destabilising.\nThe exceptions are always stable. A few patterns have quasi-sign-stability 1. Either an interaction is missing and the effects run one way only (a zero coefficient in a pair), or each pair has opposite signs, like predator and prey.\n\nFor one community, the press analysis gives the response matrix and its determinacy:\n\ns = SignedDigraph(a)\npr = press_sign_determinacy(Xoshiro(4), s; samples = 20_000)\nprintln(\"stable draws: \", round(pr.stable; digits = 2))\ngenus = first.(split.(taxa(a)))\nsigns = DataFrame(pr.sign, Symbol.(\"press \" .* genus))\ninsertcols!(signs, 1, :response => genus)\nsigns\n\nstable draws: 0.45\n\n<div><div style = \"float: left;\">4×5 DataFrame</div><div style = \"clear: both;\"></div></div><div class = \"data-frame\" style = \"overflow-x: scroll;\">\n\nRow response press Danthonia press Deschampsia press Festuca press Lolium\n SubStrin… Int8 Int8 Int8 Int8\n1 Danthonia 1 -1 1 -1\n2 Deschampsia -1 1 -1 1\n3 Festuca -1 1 1 -1\n4 Lolium 1 1 1 1\n\n</div>\n\nround.(pr.determinacy; digits = 2)\n\n4×4 Matrix{Float64}:\n 0.99  0.93  0.91  0.98\n 0.93  0.99  0.98  0.91\n 0.72  0.72  0.78  0.78\n 0.74  0.75  0.74  0.77\n\nFewer than half of the random magnitudes give a stable community with this sign pattern; the responses are computed over those.\nSome responses are well determined. The responses of Danthonia and Deschampsia have the same sign in more than 90% of the stable draws, whichever species is pressed.\nOthers are not, even to a species’ own press. For Festuca and Lolium every response has a determinacy of about 0.75, including the response to an increase in their own growth: in a quarter of the stable draws, pressing one of them lowers its own abundance through indirect effects. A determinacy near 1/2 would mean the signs say nothing.","title":"Sign patterns"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/01_plant_communities/#What-growth-rates-would-add","page":"Plant communities: structural stability and sign patterns","text":"The resilience metrics of the other vignettes are properties of the Jacobian at an equilibrium, and for a Lotka–Volterra community that is diag(N*) A, with N* = −A⁻¹r. Without r, the matrix fixes the cone of possible equilibria but not where in it the community sits. To see how much that matters, draw unit growth-rate vectors uniformly from the feasibility domain of the example community and compute the resilience of each resulting equilibrium.\n\nfunction resilience_over_r(rng, a; n = 2_000)\n    out = (resilience = Float64[], angle = Float64[])\n    while length(out.resilience) < n\n        r = normalize(randn(rng, nspecies(a)))\n        b = AlphaMatrix(matrix(a), r)\n        isfeasible(b) || continue\n        push!(out.resilience, resilience(jacobian(b)))\n        push!(out.angle, feasibility_angle(b))\n    end\n    return out\nend\nro = resilience_over_r(Xoshiro(5), a)\n(stable = mean(ro.resilience .> 0), quantiles = round.(quantile(ro.resilience, [0.05, 0.5, 0.95]); digits = 3),\n cor_angle = round(cor(ro.angle, ro.resilience); digits = 2))\n\n(stable = 1.0, quantiles = [0.008, 0.103, 0.299], cor_angle = -0.76)\n\nscatter(ro.angle, ro.resilience; xlabel = \"angle from the structural vector (degrees)\",\n        ylabel = \"resilience\", markersize = 2, alpha = 0.4, label = \"\", size = (650, 380))\n\n<div id=\"fig-r\">\n\n(Image: )\n\nFigure 3: Resilience (the slowest return rate, −max Re λ) of the example community at equilibria drawn uniformly from its feasibility domain, against the angle between r and the structural vector.\n\n</div>\n\nEvery feasible equilibrium of this community is stable, so resilience is defined throughout its feasibility domain.\nThe same interaction matrix gives very different resilience. Across feasible growth rates of unit length, the slowest return rate varies more than 30-fold between the 5th and 95th percentiles. (Resilience scales with the length of r, since N* does.)\nCommunities near the edge of their feasibility domain are the least resilient. As r approaches a boundary of the cone, one species’ equilibrium abundance goes to zero, and with it the return rate along that direction.\nSo resilience is a property of a community at an equilibrium, not of its interaction matrix. Structural stability and sign patterns are what a matrix without growth rates can support.","title":"What growth rates would add"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/01_plant_communities/#Caveats","page":"Plant communities: structural stability and sign patterns","text":"RII is not a per-capita coefficient. Reading Kinlock’s RIIs as Lotka–Volterra effects relative to self-regulation −1 is an assumption; scaling them (alpha_from_rii(net; scale)) changes Ω.\nAdler’s coefficients come from different response variables (survival, growth, biomass, population growth), and are comparable only within a study.\nThe samples are small and selected. Only complete matrices are used, which favours small communities: 14 of the 25 Kinlock networks have three species and 25 of the 39 Adler matrices have two.\nMonte Carlo error. Ω uses 2×10⁵ directions (5×10⁴ in the null comparison); the standard error is returned by feasibility_domain.","title":"Caveats"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/01_plant_communities/#References","page":"Plant communities: structural stability and sign patterns","text":"Adler, P. B. et al. (2018). Competition and coexistence in plant communities: intraspecific competition is stronger than interspecific competition. Ecology Letters 21:1319–1329. Data: doi:10.5061/dryad.q5mg97b (CC0).\nAllesina, S. & Pascual, M. (2008). Network structure, predator–prey modules, and stability in large food webs. Theoretical Ecology 1:55–64.\nDambacher, J. M., Li, H. W. & Rossignol, P. A. (2003). Qualitative predictions in model ecosystems. Ecological Modelling 161:79–93.\nKinlock, N. L. (2019). A meta-analysis of plant interaction networks reveals competitive hierarchies as well as facilitation and intransitivity. American Naturalist 194:640–653. Data: doi:10.5061/dryad.1sm06sp (CC0).\nSaavedra, S. et al. (2017). A structural approach for understanding multispecies coexistence. Ecological Monographs 87:470–486.","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/02_gut_microbiota/#A-gut-microbiota-under-antibiotics:-stability-from-a-fitted-model-and-from-data","page":"A gut microbiota under antibiotics","text":"Simon Frost\n\nOverview\nThe fitted interaction matrix\nThe community the model predicts\nStability of the predicted community\nThe clindamycin pulse in the model\nModel against observation\nStability from the time series alone\nWhat these data can and cannot validate\nCaveats\nReferences","title":"A gut microbiota under antibiotics: stability from a fitted model and from data"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/02_gut_microbiota/#Overview","page":"A gut microbiota under antibiotics","text":"Stein et al. (2013) fitted a generalised Lotka–Volterra (gLV) model to the intestinal microbiota of mice. They used 11 genera from 9 mice: some were given a single dose of clindamycin, and some were also given Clostridium difficile spores. The fit includes each genus’s susceptibility to the antibiotic. This makes it a rare empirical system with both an interaction matrix and a recorded pulse perturbation.\n\nThis vignette uses the published fit to show the package’s interaction-matrix layer:\n\nthe fitted AlphaMatrix and why its full equilibrium is not feasible;\nassembling the community the model predicts (steady_state, subcommunity, invasion_rates);\nthe stability metrics of that community (jacobian_profile);\nthe clindamycin pulse, simulated with glv_trajectory and compared with the mice;\nwhat can be estimated from the time series alone (smap_jacobians, invariability).\n\nThe conclusion is mostly negative. These data are too short, and the fit too loose, to validate resilience metrics. The vignette shows why, and what would be needed.\n\nusing CommunityMatrices\nusing DataFrames, Statistics, LinearAlgebra, Random, Printf\nusing Plots\nst = read_stein(joinpath(pkgdir(CommunityMatrices), \"data\", \"stein2013\"))\na, ε, obs = st.alpha, st.susceptibility, st.data\ngenera = taxa(a)\nshort(x) = replace(x, \"undefined_genus_of_unclassified_\" => \"u.g. \", \"undefined_genus_of_\" => \"u.g. \",\n                   \"unclassified_\" => \"uncl. \", \"Clostridium_difficile\" => \"C. difficile\", \"_\" => \" \")\n(genera = nspecies(a), samples = nrow(unique(obs[:, [:mouse, :time]])), mice = length(unique(obs.mouse)))\n\n(genera = 11, samples = 77, mice = 9)\n\nThe data are bundled in data/stein2013/ with their provenance: the published fit (Dataset S1 of the article, CC BY 4.0) and the time series (from the cLV repository, MIT). read_stein returns the fit as an AlphaMatrix, with the units and source recorded in its metadata:\n\nmatrix_metadata(a).derivation, matrix_metadata(a).units\n\n(\"Stein et al. 2013, Dataset S1 sheet MmuE (published regularised fit)\", \"per day\")","title":"Overview"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/02_gut_microbiota/#The-fitted-interaction-matrix","page":"A gut microbiota under antibiotics","text":"In the model, dNᵢ/dt = Nᵢ (rᵢ + Σⱼ Aᵢⱼ Nⱼ + εᵢ u(t)), where u(t) is the clindamycin signal. Abundances are in 10¹¹ 16S copies per cm³ and time is in days.\n\nA = matrix(a)\noff = A[.!I(nspecies(a))]\n(self_regulated = all(<(0), diag(A)), positive = count(>(0), off), negative = count(<(0), off))\n\n(self_regulated = true, positive = 45, negative = 65)\n\nlim = maximum(abs, A)\nheatmap(short.(genera), short.(genera), A; yflip = true, c = :RdBu, clims = (-lim, lim),\n        xrotation = 45, size = (720, 600), colorbar_title = \"Aᵢⱼ\")\n\n(Image: The published interaction matrix: the effect of the column genus on the per-capita growth of the row genus. Rows are sorted by clindamycin susceptibility, most harmed at the top.)\n\nDataFrame(genus = short.(genera), growth = round.(a.r; digits = 2), susceptibility = round.(ε; digits = 2))\n\n<div><div style = \"float: left;\">11×3 DataFrame</div><div style = \"clear: both;\"></div></div><div class = \"data-frame\" style = \"overflow-x: scroll;\">\n\nRow genus growth susceptibility\n String Float64 Float64\n1 Barnesiella 0.37 -3.29\n2 u.g. Lachnospiraceae 0.31 -3.04\n3 uncl. Lachnospiraceae 0.36 -2.09\n4 Other 0.54 -1.94\n5 Blautia 0.71 -1.35\n6 u.g. Mollicutes 0.47 -1.1\n7 Akkermansia 0.23 -0.92\n8 Coprobacillus 0.83 -0.79\n9 C. difficile 0.39 -0.31\n10 Enterococcus 0.29 1.07\n11 u.g. Enterobacteriaceae 0.32 3.7\n\n</div>\n\nEvery genus is self-regulated (Aᵢᵢ \\< 0).\nThe interactions are mostly negative: 65 of the 110 off-diagonal effects.\nClindamycin harms nine genera and helps two: Enterococcus and the Enterobacteriaceae genus.\n\nWith every genus present, the equilibrium N* = −A⁻¹r is not feasible:\n\n(feasible = isfeasible(a), negative_abundances = count(<(0), equilibrium(a)))\n\n(feasible = false, negative_abundances = 5)","title":"The fitted interaction matrix"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/02_gut_microbiota/#The-community-the-model-predicts","page":"A gut microbiota under antibiotics","text":"The model’s resting community comes from steady_state:\n\nit integrates from a starting state;\nremoves the genera that fall below a threshold;\nsolves for the survivors’ equilibrium;\nreintroduces any genus of the regional pool whose invasion rate is positive.\n\nThe start is the mean day-0 community of the three clindamycin-only mice. By default, the pool is the genera present at the start. These mice never received C. difficile.\n\nday0 = [mean(obs[(obs.group .== 2) .& (obs.time .== 0) .& (obs.genus .== g), :abundance]) for g in genera]\nss = steady_state(a, day0)\n(survivors = short.(genera[ss.survivors]), stable = ss.stable, uninvadable = ss.uninvadable)\n\n(survivors = [\"Other\", \"Blautia\", \"u.g. Mollicutes\", \"Akkermansia\", \"Coprobacillus\", \"u.g. Enterobacteriaceae\"], stable = true, uninvadable = true)\n\nThe surviving community has 6 genera. It is stable, and no absent genus of the pool can invade it. invasion_rates gives the per-capita growth rate of each absent genus at low density:\n\ngr = invasion_rates(a, ss.N)\nabsent = setdiff(eachindex(genera), ss.survivors)\nDataFrame(genus = short.(genera[absent]), invasion_rate = round.(gr[absent]; digits = 3),\n          present_at_day_0 = day0[absent] .> 0)\n\n<div><div style = \"float: left;\">5×3 DataFrame</div><div style = \"clear: both;\"></div></div><div class = \"data-frame\" style = \"overflow-x: scroll;\">\n\nRow genus invasion_rate presentatday_0\n String Float64 Bool\n1 Barnesiella -0.181 true\n2 u.g. Lachnospiraceae -0.191 true\n3 uncl. Lachnospiraceae -0.169 true\n4 C. difficile 0.238 false\n5 Enterococcus -0.01 true\n\n</div>\n\nThe excluded genera (Barnesiella, the two Lachnospiraceae genera and Enterococcus) have negative invasion rates.\n***C. difficile* is the exception.** Its invasion rate is positive, so it would invade. With all 11 genera in the pool, it does:\n\nshort.(genera[steady_state(a, day0; pool = eachindex(genera)).survivors])\n\n7-element Vector{String}:\n \"Other\"\n \"Blautia\"\n \"u.g. Mollicutes\"\n \"Akkermansia\"\n \"Coprobacillus\"\n \"C. difficile\"\n \"u.g. Enterobacteriaceae\"\n\nThis is a check on the fit, not only a result. The mice of population 1 were given spores without antibiotics, and C. difficile did not establish in them. At the state the fitted model assembles, the model shows no colonisation resistance. At the observed day-0 states of those mice, the model gives a mixed answer:\n\nicd = findfirst(==(\"Clostridium_difficile\"), genera)\n[(mouse = m, invasion_rate = round(invasion_rates(a, [obs[(obs.mouse .== m) .& (obs.time .== 0) .& (obs.genus .== g),\n                                                        :abundance][1] for g in genera])[icd]; digits = 3))\n for m in sort(unique(obs.mouse[obs.group .== 1]))]\n\n3-element Vector{@NamedTuple{mouse::Int64, invasion_rate::Float64}}:\n (mouse = 2, invasion_rate = 0.114)\n (mouse = 5, invasion_rate = -0.021)\n (mouse = 8, invasion_rate = 0.012)\n\nTwo of the three mice start where C. difficile could invade (rates 0.114 and 0.012 per day).\nMouse 5 starts where it could not, but only just (−0.021).\nMouse 8 carried C. difficile at a low level (about 10⁻³) throughout. Mice 2 and 5 had none.\n\nThe fit is therefore at best weakly consistent with the colonisation resistance these mice showed.","title":"The community the model predicts"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/02_gut_microbiota/#Stability-of-the-predicted-community","page":"A gut microbiota under antibiotics","text":"The survivors’ Jacobian comes with its equilibrium, so every metric applies, including the median metrics of Domínguez-García et al. (2019):\n\nsub = subcommunity(a, ss.survivors)\nJ = jacobian(sub)\nm = jacobian_profile(J)\n(Rinf = m.Rinf, return_time = 1 / m.Rinf, R0 = m.R0, Amax = m.Amax, tmax = m.tmax,\n MRinf = m.MRinf, MR0 = m.MR0, MAmax = m.MAmax)\n\n(Rinf = 0.06305951806270148, return_time = 15.858034293977283, R0 = 0.1856312718485403, Amax = 1.4519462534759764, tmax = 4.1, MRinf = 0.09108035781409002, MR0 = -0.45335049726082705, MAmax = 1.0)\n\nSlow return: the asymptotic resilience is 0.063 per day, a return time of about 16 days.\nReactive: worst-case perturbations grow at first (R0 \\> 0) and are amplified up to 1.45× after about 4 days.\nMedian pulses do not amplify (MR0 \\< 0, MAmax = 1). Here a median pulse is one scaled by abundance.","title":"Stability of the predicted community"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/02_gut_microbiota/#The-clindamycin-pulse-in-the-model","page":"A gut microbiota under antibiotics","text":"The antibiotic is a pulse: u = 1 for one day, which adds εᵢ to each genus’s per-capita growth rate. Here the pulse is applied to the model’s own equilibrium.\n\ntimes = collect(0:0.25:120.0)\npulse(t) = t < 1 ? 1.0 : 0.0\ntraj = glv_trajectory(a, ss.N, times; forcing = pulse, susceptibility = ε, tstops = [1.0])\nlg(x) = log10.(max.(x, 1e-12))\ndist = [norm(lg(traj[k, ss.survivors]) .- lg(ss.N[ss.survivors])) for k in eachindex(times)]\np1 = plot(times, lg(traj[:, ss.survivors]); label = permutedims(short.(genera[ss.survivors])), xlabel = \"day\",\n          ylabel = \"log10 abundance\", legend = :bottomright, legendfontsize = 6)\np2 = plot(times, dist; label = \"\", xlabel = \"day\", ylabel = \"distance (log10 units)\")\nvline!(p2, [1 / m.Rinf]; ls = :dash, label = \"1/Rinf\")\nplot(p1, p2; layout = (1, 2), size = (860, 340))\n\n(Image: A one-day clindamycin pulse applied to the model’s equilibrium. Left: abundances of the six genera. Right: distance from the equilibrium in log10 units, with the return time 1/Rinf marked.)\n\nat(t) = dist[findfirst(==(t), times)]\n(peak = maximum(dist), peak_day = times[argmax(dist)], day_23 = at(23.0), day_60 = at(60.0), day_120 = at(120.0))\n\n(peak = 2.7541835353664137, peak_day = 8.5, day_23 = 2.130166972847733, day_60 = 0.8851348149703921, day_120 = 0.04312846280235173)\n\nThe displacement keeps growing after the pulse ends. It peaks at about 2.8 log10 units around day 8, the transient amplification that R0 \\> 0 allows.\nRecovery takes months. The community is still 2.1 log10 units away on day 23, the last day observed in the mice, and returns only over the following months.","title":"The clindamycin pulse in the model"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/02_gut_microbiota/#Model-against-observation","page":"A gut microbiota under antibiotics","text":"For the three clindamycin-only mice, the model is started from each mouse’s observed day-0 state and given the same one-day pulse. The prediction is then compared with the observed abundances on days 2–23.\n\nfunction mouse_series(mse)\n    s = obs[obs.mouse .== mse, :]\n    ts = sort(unique(s.time))\n    X = [s[(s.time .== t) .& (s.genus .== g), :abundance][1] for t in ts, g in genera]\n    return ts, X\nend\nfl(x; floor = 1e-6) = log10.(max.(x, floor))\nplt = plot(; xlabel = \"day\", ylabel = \"distance from day 0\", legend = :bottomright)\nrows = []\nfor (k, mse) in enumerate(sort(unique(obs.mouse[obs.group .== 2])))\n    ts, X = mouse_series(mse)\n    P = glv_trajectory(a, X[1, :], ts; forcing = pulse, susceptibility = ε, tstops = [1.0])\n    dO = [norm(fl(X[i, :]) .- fl(X[1, :])) for i in eachindex(ts)]\n    dP = [norm(fl(P[i, :]) .- fl(X[1, :])) for i in eachindex(ts)]\n    plot!(plt, ts, dO; color = k, marker = :circle, label = \"mouse $mse\")\n    plot!(plt, ts, dP; color = k, ls = :dash, label = \"\")\n    late = ts .>= 2\n    o, p = vec(X[late, :]), vec(P[late, :])\n    both = (o .> 0) .& (p .> 1e-12)\n    push!(rows, (mouse = mse, cor_floor_1e4 = cor(fl(o; floor = 1e-4), fl(p; floor = 1e-4)),\n                 cor_floor_1e6 = cor(fl(o), fl(p)), cor_floor_1e8 = cor(fl(o; floor = 1e-8), fl(p; floor = 1e-8)),\n                 cor_present_only = cor(log10.(o[both]), log10.(p[both]))))\nend\nplt\n\n(Image: Distance from each mouse’s own day-0 state (log10 units, zeros floored at 10⁻⁶) for the three clindamycin-only mice: observed (solid) and predicted by the fitted model (dashed).)\n\nDataFrame(rows)\n\n<div><div style = \"float: left;\">3×5 DataFrame</div><div style = \"clear: both;\"></div></div><div class = \"data-frame\" style = \"overflow-x: scroll;\">\n\nRow mouse corfloor1e4 corfloor1e6 corfloor1e8 corpresentonly\n Int64 Float64 Float64 Float64 Float64\n1 1 0.153497 0.416137 0.632802 -0.0655451\n2 4 0.188612 0.443774 0.653775 0.0325784\n3 7 0.443673 0.58727 0.723572 0.389298\n\n</div>\n\nThe observed shift is far larger than predicted. The mice move 5–8 log10 units from their day-0 state by day 2, while the model moves about 2.7. The observed shift is driven by genera that drop to zero.\nNone recovers by day 23. The observed communities stay far from day 0. The model drifts further, towards its own equilibrium: the observed day-0 state is not the model’s equilibrium.\nThe correlation between predicted and observed log-abundances measures the zeros, not the fit.\nIt rises from about 0.15–0.44 to 0.63–0.72 as the floor for zero abundances moves from 10⁻⁴ to 10⁻⁸.\nOver the genera present in both observation and prediction, it is close to zero for two of the three mice.","title":"Model against observation"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/02_gut_microbiota/#Stability-from-the-time-series-alone","page":"A gut microbiota under antibiotics","text":"smap_jacobians estimates local Jacobians from a multivariate time series (Deyle et al. 2016), and invariability measures temporal stability as mean/sd. Each needs enough data.\n\nWhat the S-map needs.\n\nEqually spaced samples. The mice were sampled on days 0, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 9, 12, 16 and 23.\nMore time points than genera plus two. Each mouse has at most 11 points, for 11 genera.\n\nSo the method cannot be applied to these series directly. To see what it would need, apply it to series simulated from the fitted 6-genus community. Each day the community follows the model, and is then multiplied by log-normal process noise (5% on the log scale).\n\nfunction noisy(rng, sub, N0, T; σ = 0.05)\n    X = zeros(T, length(N0))\n    x = copy(N0)\n    for t in 1:T\n        X[t, :] = x\n        x = glv_trajectory(sub, x, [0.0, 1.0])[end, :] .* exp.(σ .* randn(rng, length(x)))\n    end\n    return X\nend\nXsim = noisy(Xoshiro(1), sub, ss.N[ss.survivors], 500)\nJs = smap_jacobians(Xsim; theta = 1.0, times = 101:20:481)\nlong = [resilience(j) for j in Js]\n# error of the whole matrix, against the model's Jacobian at the same state\njac_error = [opnorm(j.J - jacobian(sub; N = j.equilibrium).J) / opnorm(jacobian(sub; N = j.equilibrium).J) for j in Js]\nshort_windows = [resilience(smap_jacobians(Xsim[s:(s + 10), :]; theta = 0.0, times = [10])[1]) for s in 1:10:480]\n(true_Rinf = m.Rinf, from_500_days = median(long), relative_error_of_J = median(jac_error),\n from_11_days_median = median(short_windows), from_11_days_range = extrema(short_windows))\n\n(true_Rinf = 0.06305951806270148, from_500_days = 0.06811233948466101, relative_error_of_J = 0.9268124981741833, from_11_days_median = -0.007026155025189483, from_11_days_range = (-0.5754211286062723, 0.41343949650662937))\n\nplot([fill(1, length(long)); fill(2, length(short_windows))], [long; short_windows]; seriestype = :scatter,\n     xticks = ([1, 2], [\"500 daily points\", \"11 daily points\"]), xlims = (0.5, 2.5), label = \"\",\n     ylabel = \"estimated Rinf (per day)\", alpha = 0.6, size = (520, 380))\nhline!([m.Rinf]; ls = :dash, label = \"true Rinf\")\n\n(Image: Resilience estimated by the S-map from simulated series: 20 target times in one 500-day series (θ = 1), and 48 separate 11-day windows (θ = 0). The dashed line is the true resilience of the model’s equilibrium.)\n\nFrom 500 daily points, the S-map recovers the dominant rate of return to within about 10%.\nThe full Jacobian is recovered much less well. The error in its norm is about as large as the matrix itself: small noise excites some directions too little for their entries to be estimated.\nFrom 11 daily points, the estimates scatter widely around zero: they cannot even tell whether the community is stable.\n\nInvariability needs no model, so it can be computed on the mice directly:\n\ninvtab = combine(groupby(obs, [:mouse, :group]), [:time, :abundance] => ((t, x) -> begin\n                  tot = [sum(x[t .== s]) for s in sort(unique(t))]\n                  (points = length(tot), invariability = invariability(tot))\n              end) => AsTable)\nsort!(invtab, [:group, :mouse])\ninvtab.treatment = getindex.(Ref(Dict(1 => \"spores only\", 2 => \"clindamycin\", 3 => \"clindamycin + spores\")), invtab.group)\ninvtab\n\n<div><div style = \"float: left;\">9×5 DataFrame</div><div style = \"clear: both;\"></div></div><div class = \"data-frame\" style = \"overflow-x: scroll;\">\n\nRow mouse group points invariability treatment\n Int64 Int64 Int64 Float64 String\n1 2 1 4 5.19644 spores only\n2 5 1 4 4.70595 spores only\n3 8 1 4 2.88673 spores only\n4 1 2 11 0.774032 clindamycin\n5 4 2 11 1.01702 clindamycin\n6 7 2 11 1.17662 clindamycin\n7 3 3 10 0.840661 clindamycin + spores\n8 6 3 11 1.11215 clindamycin + spores\n9 9 3 11 0.975551 clindamycin + spores\n\n</div>\n\nUntreated mice are steadier. The total abundance of the mice that received no antibiotic varies less (invariability 2.9–5.2) than that of treated mice (0.8–1.2).\nBut this measures the perturbation, not an intrinsic property. The treated mice were knocked far from their state, and the untreated ones have only four samples each.\nIn the simulation, process noise alone gives invariability near 30.","title":"Stability from the time series alone"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/02_gut_microbiota/#What-these-data-can-and-cannot-validate","page":"A gut microbiota under antibiotics","text":"They can check the fit’s qualitative claims. For example, the fitted model allows C. difficile to invade the community it assembles, which the mice of population 1 do not support.\nThey cannot validate return times. The predicted return time (about 16 days, longer after the amplification) is as long as the whole series. The observed communities had not recovered when sampling stopped.\nThey cannot support data-driven Jacobians. That would need long, evenly spaced series with many more samples than taxa. Long perturbation studies such as MDSINE2 (Bucci et al.\ncome closer.","title":"What these data can and cannot validate"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/02_gut_microbiota/#Caveats","page":"A gut microbiota under antibiotics","text":"Zeros. About 9% of the abundances are exactly zero. Log-scale distances and correlations depend on how zeros are floored; this vignette uses 10⁻⁶ and shows the sensitivity.\nOne published fit. The fit is regularised (Stein et al. 2013, Dataset S1), and its uncertainty is not used here. Refitting is outside the scope of this package.\nThe pulse shape. The clindamycin signal is 1 on the day of the dose and 0 afterwards. A one-day pulse is the simplest reading of that signal.","title":"Caveats"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/02_gut_microbiota/#References","page":"A gut microbiota under antibiotics","text":"Bucci V. et al. (2016) MDSINE: Microbial Dynamical Systems INference Engine for microbiome time-series analyses. Genome Biology 17:121.\nDeyle E.R., May R.M., Munch S.B. & Sugihara G. (2016) Tracking and forecasting ecosystem interactions in real time. Proc. R. Soc. B 283:20152258.\nDomínguez-García V., Dakos V. & Kéfi S. (2019) Unveiling dimensions of stability in complex ecological networks. PNAS 116:25714–25720.\nStein R.R. et al. (2013) Ecological modeling from time-series inference: insight into dynamics and stability of intestinal microbiota. PLoS Comput Biol 9:e1003388.","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"api/matrices/#Interaction-matrices-and-Lotka–Volterra","page":"Interaction matrices and Lotka–Volterra","text":"Typed matrices make explicit which object a number belongs to, and conversions between them ask for what they need.","title":"Interaction matrices and Lotka–Volterra"},{"category":"section","location":"api/matrices/#Assembly-and-trajectories","page":"Interaction matrices and Lotka–Volterra","text":"","title":"Assembly and trajectories"},{"category":"type","location":"api/matrices/#CommunityMatrices.MatrixMetadata","page":"Interaction matrices and Lotka–Volterra","text":"MatrixMetadata(; taxa = String[], state_variables, units, sign_convention, spatial_bounds,\n                 temporal_bounds, derivation, uncertainty, software, licence)\n\nThe minimal metadata record proposed for deposited interaction matrices (interaction-matrix review; Terry et al. 2025): the species (taxa, in row order), what the state variables are, units, the sign convention, spatial and temporal bounds, how the entries were derived, how their uncertainty is represented, the software used and the licence. All fields are strings except taxa; unknown fields default to \"unspecified\".\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityMatrices.MatrixMetadata"},{"category":"type","location":"api/matrices/#CommunityMatrices.InteractionMatrix","page":"Interaction matrices and Lotka–Volterra","text":"InteractionMatrix\n\nSupertype of the typed interaction matrices: AlphaMatrix, Jacobian, CommunityMatrix, FluxMatrix, SignedDigraph and ProbabilisticNetwork. Use matrix for the numbers, matrix_metadata and taxa for the record, and size/nspecies for the dimension.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityMatrices.InteractionMatrix"},{"category":"function","location":"api/matrices/#CommunityMatrices.matrix","page":"Interaction matrices and Lotka–Volterra","text":"matrix(x::InteractionMatrix)\n\nThe numeric matrix held by x (rows are affected species, columns are acting species).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityMatrices.matrix"},{"category":"function","location":"api/matrices/#CommunityMatrices.matrix_metadata","page":"Interaction matrices and Lotka–Volterra","text":"matrix_metadata(x::InteractionMatrix)\n\nThe MatrixMetadata record of x.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityMatrices.matrix_metadata"},{"category":"function","location":"api/matrices/#CommunityMatrices.taxa","page":"Interaction matrices and Lotka–Volterra","text":"taxa(x::InteractionMatrix)\n\nSpecies names in row order (\"sp1\", \"sp2\", … if none were given).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityMatrices.taxa"},{"category":"function","location":"api/matrices/#CommunityMatrices.nspecies","page":"Interaction matrices and Lotka–Volterra","text":"nspecies(x::InteractionMatrix)\n\nThe number of species (rows) of x.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityMatrices.nspecies"},{"category":"type","location":"api/matrices/#CommunityMatrices.AlphaMatrix","page":"Interaction matrices and Lotka–Volterra","text":"AlphaMatrix(A, r; meta = MatrixMetadata())\n\nGeneralised Lotka–Volterra coefficients: dNᵢ/dt = Nᵢ (rᵢ + Σⱼ Aᵢⱼ Nⱼ), with per-capita effects A (negative diagonal for self-regulation) and intrinsic growth rates r. Other parameterisations (e.g. annual-plant λᵢ/(1 + Σⱼ αᵢⱼ Nⱼ)) must be converted first; the conversion belongs in meta.derivation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityMatrices.AlphaMatrix"},{"category":"function","location":"api/matrices/#CommunityMatrices.glv_rhs!","page":"Interaction matrices and Lotka–Volterra","text":"glv_rhs!(du, N, a::AlphaMatrix, t)\n\nThe generalised Lotka–Volterra vector field of a in the in-place SciML form.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityMatrices.glv_rhs!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/matrices/#CommunityMatrices.equilibrium","page":"Interaction matrices and Lotka–Volterra","text":"equilibrium(a::AlphaMatrix)\n\nThe interior equilibrium N* = −A⁻¹ r (all species present), which may have negative entries.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityMatrices.equilibrium"},{"category":"function","location":"api/matrices/#CommunityMatrices.isfeasible","page":"Interaction matrices and Lotka–Volterra","text":"isfeasible(a::AlphaMatrix)\n\nWhether the interior equilibrium of a has every species at positive abundance.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityMatrices.isfeasible"},{"category":"type","location":"api/matrices/#CommunityMatrices.Jacobian","page":"Interaction matrices and Lotka–Volterra","text":"Jacobian(J, equilibrium = Float64[]; meta = MatrixMetadata())\n\nThe Jacobian (population-level community matrix) of a dynamical system at an equilibrium, with that equilibrium when known. All Jacobian-based metrics accept it; the median metrics then use pulses proportional to equilibrium.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityMatrices.Jacobian"},{"category":"method","location":"api/matrices/#CommunityMatrices.Jacobian-Tuple{ContinuousJacobian}","page":"Interaction matrices and Lotka–Volterra","text":"Jacobian(op::ContinuousJacobian; meta = MatrixMetadata())\nContinuousJacobian(j::Jacobian)\n\nConvert between this package's typed Jacobian, which carries metadata, and ResilienceCore's ContinuousJacobian, on which the metrics are defined. A community that implements ResilienceCore's interface (for example an assembled food web of FoodWebDynamics.jl) gives its Jacobian as Jacobian(linearise(c)).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityMatrices.Jacobian"},{"category":"type","location":"api/matrices/#CommunityMatrices.CommunityMatrix","page":"Interaction matrices and Lotka–Volterra","text":"CommunityMatrix(M, equilibrium; meta = MatrixMetadata())\n\nPer-capita effects at an equilibrium: Mᵢⱼ = ∂(Ṅᵢ/Nᵢ)/∂Nⱼ. For Lotka–Volterra it is the coefficient matrix A. Convert with jacobian(m) = diag(N*) M and per_capita(J).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityMatrices.CommunityMatrix"},{"category":"function","location":"api/matrices/#CommunityMatrices.per_capita","page":"Interaction matrices and Lotka–Volterra","text":"per_capita(j::Jacobian)\n\nThe per-capita CommunityMatrix diag(N*)⁻¹ J; needs the equilibrium, and every species present.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityMatrices.per_capita"},{"category":"type","location":"api/matrices/#CommunityMatrices.FluxMatrix","page":"Interaction matrices and Lotka–Volterra","text":"FluxMatrix(F, biomass; meta = MatrixMetadata())\n\nFeeding fluxes at a steady state: F[i, j] is the rate at which consumer i removes biomass of resource j (e.g. from Ecopath or fluxweb), with the standing biomass of every species. jacobian turns it into a Jacobian under stated assumptions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityMatrices.FluxMatrix"},{"category":"method","location":"api/matrices/#ResilienceCore.jacobian-Tuple{AlphaMatrix}","page":"Interaction matrices and Lotka–Volterra","text":"jacobian(a::AlphaMatrix; N = equilibrium(a))\n\nThe Jacobian of the Lotka–Volterra system a at abundances N: diag(N) A at an interior equilibrium (for other N the general form diag(r + A N) + diag(N) A is used).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ResilienceCore.jacobian"},{"category":"method","location":"api/matrices/#ResilienceCore.jacobian-Tuple{FluxMatrix}","page":"Interaction matrices and Lotka–Volterra","text":"jacobian(f::FluxMatrix; efficiency, self_regulation)\n\nThe Jacobian implied by steady-state fluxes when feeding is mass action, Fᵢⱼ = aᵢⱼ Bᵢ Bⱼ (de Ruiter et al. 1995): the effect of resource j on consumer i is eⱼ Fᵢⱼ / Bⱼ, the effect of consumer j on resource i is −Fⱼᵢ / Bⱼ, and the diagonal is −self_regulation (which fluxes cannot determine). efficiency is a vector over resources (or a scalar); self_regulation a vector (or scalar) of nonnegative rates.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ResilienceCore.jacobian"},{"category":"type","location":"api/matrices/#CommunityMatrices.SignedDigraph","page":"Interaction matrices and Lotka–Volterra","text":"SignedDigraph(S; meta = MatrixMetadata())\nSignedDigraph(x::InteractionMatrix)\n\nA sign pattern (entries −1, 0, +1) of the effects of species on each other, as used in loop analysis and qualitative press-perturbation analysis. The second form takes the signs of the Jacobian or community matrix of x.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityMatrices.SignedDigraph"},{"category":"type","location":"api/matrices/#CommunityMatrices.ProbabilisticNetwork","page":"Interaction matrices and Lotka–Volterra","text":"ProbabilisticNetwork(P; meta = MatrixMetadata())\n\nProbabilities P[i, j] that species j affects species i (for example, that i eats j), e.g. from sampling-corrected or predicted links. rand(rng, p) draws a BitMatrix of links.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityMatrices.ProbabilisticNetwork"},{"category":"function","location":"api/matrices/#CommunityMatrices.subcommunity","page":"Interaction matrices and Lotka–Volterra","text":"subcommunity(a::AlphaMatrix, idx)\n\nThe Lotka–Volterra system of the species idx of a alone.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityMatrices.subcommunity"},{"category":"function","location":"api/matrices/#CommunityMatrices.invasion_rates","page":"Interaction matrices and Lotka–Volterra","text":"invasion_rates(a::AlphaMatrix, N)\n\nPer-capita growth rates r + A N of every species at abundances N; for species absent from N these are their invasion growth rates.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityMatrices.invasion_rates"},{"category":"function","location":"api/matrices/#CommunityMatrices.steady_state","page":"Interaction matrices and Lotka–Volterra","text":"steady_state(a::AlphaMatrix, N0; pool = findall(>(0), N0), tmax = 1e4, threshold = 1e-8,\n             reseed = 1e-6, rounds = 20)\n\nThe assembled state of the Lotka–Volterra system a from N0: integrate to tmax, remove species below threshold, and solve for the survivors' interior equilibrium; while some species of the regional pool (by default those present in N0) could invade (positive invasion rate), reintroduce them at density reseed and repeat (up to rounds times). Returns (N, survivors, stable, uninvadable): the equilibrium (zeros for absent species), the survivors' indices, whether their Jacobian is stable, and whether every absent species of the pool has a negative invasion rate.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityMatrices.steady_state"},{"category":"function","location":"api/matrices/#CommunityMatrices.glv_trajectory","page":"Interaction matrices and Lotka–Volterra","text":"glv_trajectory(a::AlphaMatrix, N0, times; forcing = nothing, susceptibility = nothing,\n               tstops = Float64[])\n\nAbundances of the Lotka–Volterra system a at times from N0 at times[1], optionally with a perturbation that adds susceptibility[i] × forcing(t) to species i's per-capita growth rate (the antibiotic model of Stein et al. 2013); give the forcing's switching times as tstops. Returns a matrix with one row per time.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityMatrices.glv_trajectory"},{"category":"section","location":"api/timeseries/#Time-series","page":"Time series","text":"","title":"Time series"},{"category":"function","location":"api/timeseries/#CommunityMatrices.smap_jacobians","page":"Time series","text":"smap_jacobians(X; theta = 2.0, ridge = 0.0, dt = 1.0, times = 1:size(X, 1) - 1,\n               continuous = true, meta = MatrixMetadata())\n\nLocal Jacobians from a multivariate time series X (rows are times at spacing dt, columns species) by the S-map. For each target time t*, every xᵢ(t + 1) is regressed linearly on x(t) (with intercept), weighting each past state by exp(−theta d / d̄), where d is its distance from x(t*) and d̄ the mean distance; ridge > 0 adds an L2 penalty on the slopes. The slopes form the one-step map Jacobian Φ(t*). With continuous = true it is converted to a continuous-time Jacobian by log(Φ)/dt (real part); otherwise Φ is returned in a Jacobian (map) and only its spectral radius is meaningful for stability. theta = 0 gives one global linear (VAR(1)) fit. Returns a vector of Jacobians, one per element of times, each with the state x(t*) as its equilibrium field (a reference state, not an equilibrium).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityMatrices.smap_jacobians"},{"category":"function","location":"api/timeseries/#CommunityMatrices.invariability","page":"Time series","text":"invariability(x)\n\nTemporal invariability mean(x) / std(x) of a series (Haegeman et al. 2016), e.g. of total community biomass; the data counterpart of stochastic invariability.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityMatrices.invariability"},{"category":"section","location":"api/data/#Empirical-data","page":"Empirical data","text":"Each reader records its conversions in the matrices' metadata. The data ship in data/, each with a README giving its source, licence and checksums:\n\ndata/kinlock2019: plant interaction networks of Kinlock (2019), CC0;\ndata/adler2018: competition coefficients compiled by Adler et al. (2018), CC0;\ndata/stein2013: the gut-microbiota model and data of Stein et al. (2013), CC BY 4.0 (the article's supporting information) and MIT (the cLV repository's copy).","title":"Empirical data"},{"category":"function","location":"api/data/#CommunityMatrices.read_kinlock","page":"Empirical data","text":"read_kinlock(path; control = :monoculture, complete = true)\n\nPlant interaction networks of Kinlock (2019, Dryad doi:10.5061/dryad.1sm06sp, CC0): for each study, a matrix of relative interaction indices (RII ∈ [−1, 1]; entry (i, j) the effect of species j on species i, negative for competition), against monoculture controls (control = :monoculture, column NetworkMonoCtrl, diagonal 0 by construction) or plants grown alone (:alone, NetworkTrueCtrl). With complete = true only networks without missing entries are returned. Returns a vector of (study, species, rii, habitat).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityMatrices.read_kinlock"},{"category":"function","location":"api/data/#CommunityMatrices.alpha_from_rii","page":"Empirical data","text":"alpha_from_rii(net; scale = 1.0)\n\nAn AlphaMatrix from a Kinlock network: off-diagonal per-capita effects scale × RII relative to self-regulation −1. RII values are relative changes in performance, not per-capita coefficients, so this is an assumption, recorded in the metadata; the growth rates are unknown and set to 1, which affects equilibria but not the feasibility domain.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityMatrices.alpha_from_rii"},{"category":"function","location":"api/data/#CommunityMatrices.read_adler","page":"Empirical data","text":"read_adler(path; normalise = true)\n\nComplete competition-coefficient matrices from the database of Adler et al. (2018, Ecology Letters, Dryad doi:10.5061/dryad.q5mg97b, CC0): rows grouped by study, response variable and treatment, kept when every focal × competitor pair (including intraspecific) is present. Signs are put in the Lotka–Volterra convention (negative = competition) using the column \"Negative coefs mean competition\" (Yes/No; other entries skip the group). With normalise = true each row is divided by the absolute intraspecific coefficient, so the diagonal is −1. Growth rates are not in the database (set to 1). Returns a vector of (study, response, treatment, alpha::AlphaMatrix).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityMatrices.read_adler"},{"category":"function","location":"api/data/#CommunityMatrices.read_stein","page":"Empirical data","text":"read_stein(dir)\n\nThe gut-microbiota generalised Lotka–Volterra model and data of Stein et al. (2013, PLoS Comput Biol 9:e1003388): dir holds derived/stein2013_glv.csv (the published fit, sheet MmuE of their Dataset S1) and clv_data_stein/raw_data.csv (the time series, MIT-licensed copy from the cLV repository). Returns (alpha, susceptibility, data): the published AlphaMatrix (rows and columns in the sheet's order), the clindamycin susceptibilities ε (a pulse u(t) adds εᵢ u(t) to the per-capita growth of species i), and a long DataFrame with columns mouse, group, time, genus, abundance (10¹¹ 16S copies per cm³) and clindamycin.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityMatrices.read_stein"},{"category":"section","location":"#CommunityMatrices.jl","page":"Home","text":"Interaction matrices of ecological communities, and what they say about stability.\n\n\"Interaction matrix\" names several different objects: Lotka–Volterra coefficients, the Jacobian at an equilibrium, the per-capita community matrix, feeding fluxes and sign patterns. Software written for one silently accepts another. Here each is its own type, conversions between them are explicit functions that ask for what they need, and every matrix carries a metadata record of where it came from.\n\nTyped matrices: AlphaMatrix, Jacobian, CommunityMatrix, FluxMatrix, SignedDigraph and ProbabilisticNetwork, with MatrixMetadata.\nLotka–Volterra assembly: equilibria and feasibility, subcommunities, invasion rates, steady states reached from a species pool, and trajectories under a forcing such as an antibiotic pulse.\nStructural stability: the size of the feasibility domain and the angle of the growth rates from its centre.\nSign patterns: quasi-sign-stability and qualitative press responses.\nTime series: local Jacobians by the S-map, and temporal invariability.\nEmpirical data: readers and bundled data for the Kinlock (2019) and Adler et al. (2018) plant interaction matrices and the Stein et al. (2013) gut-microbiota model.\n\nThe resilience metrics come from ResilienceCore.jl, which defines them on operators of every kind (equilibria, projection matrices, periodic and Markov operators) and is re-exported here. Its documentation also lists the resilience tutorials of the other ecorecipes packages.\n\nusing CommunityMatrices\n\na = AlphaMatrix([-1.0 -0.4; -0.3 -1.0], [1.0, 0.8])   # Lotka–Volterra: dN/dt = N (r + A N)\nisfeasible(a), equilibrium(a)\nfeasibility_domain(a)                                   # (Ω, Ω^(1/S), se)\nJ = jacobian(a)                                         # a typed Jacobian at the equilibrium\nresilience(J), reactivity(J), max_amplification(J)\nquasi_sign_stability(SignedDigraph(J))","title":"CommunityMatrices.jl"},{"category":"module","location":"#CommunityMatrices","page":"Home","text":"CommunityMatrices\n\nInteraction matrices of ecological communities, and what can be said about stability from them.\n\nTyped matrices. \"Interaction matrix\" names several objects: Lotka–Volterra coefficients (AlphaMatrix), the Jacobian at an equilibrium (Jacobian), per-capita community matrices (CommunityMatrix), feeding fluxes (FluxMatrix), sign patterns (SignedDigraph) and link probabilities (ProbabilisticNetwork). Each is its own type, conversions between them ask for what they need, and every matrix carries a MatrixMetadata record of where it came from.\nLotka–Volterra assembly: equilibria, feasibility, subcommunities, invasion rates, steady states reached from a species pool, and forced trajectories.\nStructural stability: the feasibility domain and the angle from its centre (Saavedra et al. 2017).\nSign patterns: quasi-sign-stability and qualitative press responses.\nTime series: local Jacobians by the S-map, and temporal invariability.\nEmpirical data: readers for the Kinlock (2019) and Adler et al. (2018) plant interaction matrices and the Stein et al. (2013) gut-microbiota model, with the data.\n\nThe resilience metrics themselves (resilience, reactivity, max_amplification, the median metrics, stochastic_invariability, the sensitivities, and jacobian_profile for all eleven at once) come from ResilienceCore.jl and are re-exported; they accept a Jacobian directly, through its ContinuousJacobian.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityMatrices"}]
}
